# One-command build orchestration

Implement `tools/build/auto.py` as an optional one-command user flow that runs
dependency bootstrap, preflight, architecture selection, and build in order
while persisting each decision JSON so repeated or interrupted runs use
explicit evidence instead of hidden process state.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Run `dependencies.py`, `check.py`, `arch.py`, and `run.py` in declared
  order without duplicating their domain logic.
- [x] Persist all decisions and validation evidence beneath `.cache/build/data/`
  with versioned schemas.
- [x] Revalidate saved evidence on resume and never trust stale paths, hashes,
  or tool versions implicitly.
- [x] Keep ordinary user interaction limited to actionable errors and the
  architecture checklist.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency is required beyond the declared repository and
platform prerequisites.

## Evidence

`tools/build/auto.py` invokes `dependencies.py`, `check.py`, `arch.py`, then
`run.py` with the current exact Python and stops immediately on any failed child
step. It does not duplicate child validation or packaging policy. An existing
`arch.json` is passed through `arch.py --revalidate`; a missing decision opens
the normal checklist, while `--reselect` explicitly requests a new selection.

A real Windows AMD64 `auto.py --validate-only` run completed all four canonical
steps, including the repo-local dependency bootstrap and real game/Unreal
preflight,
saved architecture revalidation, and Turnkey SDK verification. The temporary
architecture decision used for that integration test was removed afterward, so
no test target remained as user state. Unit fixtures also verified command
order,
resume behavior, and `--validate-only` forwarding.
